mk("WeOuxcN0pV8","How I Actually Use Euclidean Sequencing on Digitone 2",
 "dual-euclidean-pulse-generators-boolean-or-xor-and-sub-rotation-track-rotate-print-to-steps-melodic-redistribute-arp-fill",
 "Two Euclidean pulse generators per track combined with Boolean logic (OR, XOR, AND, SUB), per-generator rotation and whole-track rotation, then printed to an ordinary step sequence for hand edits; also used to redistribute an existing bassline and to leave gaps an arpeggiator fills",
 "Generate musically coherent, non-obvious drum and melodic patterns by combining two Euclidean pulse generators on one track. Each generator spreads N pulses as evenly as possible over the track length (e.g. 16 or 32 steps); numbers that do not divide the length (5, 9, 11, 12) give the interesting grooves. Combine the two with a Boolean operator: OR (any pulse plays; overlaps become one note), XOR (overlaps are removed, leaving space), AND (only overlaps play; the number of possible notes is limited by the smaller pulse count) or SUB (generator 1 minus where it overlaps generator 2, a way to carve notes out). Rotate each generator to change their relationship, then rotate the whole track to move the downbeat of the combined pattern. Print the result to a normal step sequence to edit single notes, apply the same Euclidean distribution to a pre-programmed melodic line (notes stay, positions change), randomise the Euclidean settings with a snapshot to return to, and let an arpeggiator with step offsets fill the gaps. Delta beyond our probability/seeded loop skills: the two-generator Boolean combine plus print-to-steps workflow, mapped to Polyrhythm Studio lanes.",
 ["Step sequencer or DAW/MIDI tool that can compute Euclidean patterns, or Web Audio / JS: compute Bjorklund patterns for two (pulses, length, rotation) sets and combine the boolean arrays per step (implementation note)","Drum and bass voices; optional arpeggiator"],
 [("length","integer","Track length in steps (16 or 32 in the video)",True),("pulses_1","integer","Pulses for generator 1",True),("pulses_2","integer","Pulses for generator 2",True),("operator","enum","OR | XOR | AND | SUB",True),("rotation_1","integer","Rotation of generator 1",False),("rotation_2","integer","Rotation of generator 2",False),("track_rotation","integer","Rotation applied to the combined pattern",False)],
 [("pattern","list","Combined boolean step pattern per track"),("printed_sequence","pattern","Ordinary step sequence after printing, ready for hand edits")],
 ["AND with no overlaps is silent; raise pulse counts or rotate until hits appear.","Printing converts the pattern to fixed steps; Euclidean edits after that no longer apply unless you re-enable it.","Device button combos are product-specific and omitted; use any Euclidean tool. No numbers beyond pulses, length and rotations were given."],
 [("Turn on Euclidean mode for a drum track. Set generator 1 to a simple count (e.g. 4 pulses over 16 steps) and listen; then try counts that do not divide 16 (5, 11).","euclid gen 1","Evenly spread hits; odd counts give syncopated grooves"),
  ("Enable generator 2 with a different count (e.g. 5 against 4) using OR. Rotate generator 2 to move its hits against generator 1.","euclid gen 2 + rotation","An overlaid polyrhythmic feel that changes with rotation; overlaps count as one note"),
  ("Switch the operator: XOR to remove overlapping hits and open space; AND to keep only overlaps (raise counts, e.g. 9 and 12, and rotate until notes appear); SUB to keep generator 1 except where generator 2 lands.","boolean operator","Same two generators give four different densities and feels"),
  ("When an emergent pattern makes a new downbeat, use whole-track rotation (e.g. +3 steps) to move both generators together so that hit becomes step 1.","track rotation","The pattern starts where you want without changing the inner relationship"),
  ("Print the pattern to the normal step sequencer, then hand-edit (move a note, leave room for a snare). Build hats on another track at length 32 with SUB to carve notes out of a dense generator 1.","print + edit","Fixed, editable steps keep the Euclidean groove"),
  ("On a melodic (bass) track with notes already programmed, enable Euclidean mode so those notes are redistributed by the pulse pattern; add generator 2 with XOR and rotate the track. Randomise the Euclidean settings repeatedly until a version works.","melodic redistribute","Same notes in new rhythmic positions"),
  ("For sparse patterns, enable an arpeggiator and remove one arp note with its offset/step mask so it fills gaps without crowding.","arp fill","Gaps filled with a grooving arp line"),
  ("For performance, change rotations and pulses on all tracks at once, and keep a snapshot so you can jump back to the original pattern.","live control","Patterns tangle and untangle; one action returns to the saved state")],
 ["For pulses 4 and 5 over 16 steps, OR yields more hits than XOR, and AND yields only the shared positions.","Changing rotation of one generator changes the combined pattern while total pulse counts stay the same.","After printing, editing one step changes only that step.","The redistributed bassline keeps its original pitches."],
 ["euclidean","sequencer","boolean-logic","polyrhythm","rotation","generative","drums","polyrhythm-studio","web-audio"],
 note="Hardware walkthrough; device button combos and part-two teaser dropped.")

mk("mb3yqXO9JiE","Part 2: Generative Sequencing for Techno (or any other style of music)",
 "generative-rhythmic-sequence-euclid-length-phase-against-step-count-chance-rests-random-octave-degree-random-accent-release-smooth-random-filter-wt",
 "Generative rhythmic sequence from a hand-written 8-step pattern: live length and start-point changes, chance-based rests, a 5-step Euclidean trigger length phasing against the 8 steps (a 40-step cycle), random modulation of octave and scale degree kept subtle, random distortion and release for accents and note lengths, smooth-random slow LFOs on filter and wavetable position, introduced gradually in performance",
 "Turn a short, intentional sequence into an evolving techno or ambient line without writing new notes. Start from a programmed 8-step pattern (or, for pure random, a one-step root note with random → scale degree attenuated to about 7–8 degrees, which gives quantised random melodies within one octave). Vary it by changing pattern length and start point while it plays. Add rests with chance (about 75% means roughly one in four steps silent) and/or a Euclidean trigger length that does not divide the step count (5 against 8): with chance slightly under 100% (about 90%) the 5-step trigger pattern shifts across the 8 notes and the cycle repeats only after 40 steps. Add a tempo-synced delay so echoes fill the rests. Then add pitch variation by patching random to octave (positive for up, negative for down jumps) and to scale degree (jumps of about 4–5 degrees), keeping both subtle so the line's shape survives. Add sound variation: random → distortion (accents) and random → release (note lengths, with a lower base release), and slow smooth-random LFOs (around 5 s) on filter cutoff and wavetable position. Don't use everything at once; bring modulations in gradually across a set.",
 ["Sequencer with chance, Euclidean length and per-step random modulation (the video uses a small hardware synth), or Web Audio / JS: a step clock, a Bjorklund trigger mask of length 5 indexed mod 5 while notes are indexed mod 8, Math.random() gates, and scale quantising (implementation note)","Synth voice with distortion, release, filter and wavetable position as modulation targets","Tempo-synced delay"],
 [("steps","list","Programmed notes (8 in the video)",True),("chance","number","Probability each step plays, 0–100%",True),("euclid_length","integer","Trigger pattern length, preferably not a divisor of the step count (5)",False),("degree_random","number","Random → scale degree depth (≈4–5 degrees subtle; 7–8 = one octave)",False),("octave_random","number","Random → octave depth (sign sets up or down)",False)],
 [("generative_sequence","patch","Sequence + modulation routing"),("performance_plan","list","Order in which modulations are introduced during a set")],
 ["Chance only creates rests with the Euclidean trigger if chance is below 100%.","Wavetable-position modulation only works when its base value is above zero (in the video's synth, negative values switch to pulse width and modulation cannot pass through zero).","Heavy random on both octave and degree turns the line into noise; keep it subtle.","Firmware-specific behaviour and device pad names omitted; values are the presenter's approximations."],
 [("Program an 8-step sequence (or one root step plus random → scale degree around 7–8 degrees for an instant quantised random melody). Save it as the base.","base sequence","A repeating line that stays in key"),
  ("While playing, change pattern length and set a different start point to get variation from the same notes (e.g. steps 1–16 vs 17–32 for different song sections).","length/start","New phrase shapes without new notes"),
  ("Set chance to about 75% for random rests. Alternatively set a Euclidean trigger length of 5 with chance about 90%, so the 5-step trigger pattern drifts across the 8 notes.","chance + euclid length","Rests appear; with 5 vs 8 the pattern repeats only every 40 steps"),
  ("Add a tempo-synced delay so echoes land in the rests.","timed delay","Rests filled by in-time echoes"),
  ("Patch random → octave (positive = up jumps, negative = down) and random → scale degree, attenuating until jumps of about 4–5 degrees appear. Optionally map degree to a knob to sweep between bassline and lead register.","pitch random","Occasional octave and degree jumps while the line keeps its shape"),
  ("Patch random → distortion for accents and random → release for varied note lengths; lower the base release so variation is audible.","sound random","Uneven accents and note lengths"),
  ("Add LFO X (smooth random, slow) → filter cutoff and LFO Y (smooth random, about 5 s) → wavetable position, with base position above zero.","slow LFOs","Slow tonal drift even without touching controls"),
  ("When performing, start with the plain sequence and introduce degree, octave and timbre modulation one by one over time.","arrangement","The piece develops instead of starting at full chaos")],
 ["With euclid length 5 over 8 steps and chance <100%, the triggered note set differs on successive passes and repeats after 40 steps.","At chance 75%, about a quarter of steps are silent over many loops.","With degree random subtle, most notes stay close to the original line.","Filter and wavetable modulation are audible over 5–10 s without any manual change."],
 ["generative","sequencer","euclidean","probability","random-modulation","polymeter","techno","ambient","polyrhythm-studio","web-audio"],
 note="Hardware walkthrough; firmware-version, Discord, release-pricing talk and the closing jam (music only) dropped.")

mk("UchJGizLiLM","Euclidean Weaver v1.2.0 | Generate Euclidean Rhythms and Melodies in Bitwig Studio",
 "eight-layer-euclidean-weaver-drum-or-scale-melody-mode-one-scale-note-per-layer-steps-pulses-rotation-gate-drift-randomize-keep-rhythm-change-scale",
 "Eight-layer Euclidean pattern generator with two modes: drums (each layer = one MIDI drum note) and melody (each layer = a different note of a chosen scale and root, so stacked layers form in-key chords); per-layer steps, pulses, rotation, velocity and gate, a drift action that nudges each layer's pulses by ±1, layer or global randomise that never changes note assignment, and scale/root changes that keep the rhythm",
 "Write in-key polyrhythmic melodies and grooves by stacking up to eight Euclidean layers. Each layer has steps (pattern length), pulses (hits spread as evenly as possible), rotation (offset, which shifts layers against each other), velocity and gate (note length). In drum mode each layer triggers its own drum note; in melody mode each layer is assigned a different note from a chosen scale and root, so layers of different lengths interlock into melodies and coinciding hits make chords, always in key. Generate the result into a clip, add layers one at a time, use drift to randomly move each active layer's pulse count up or down by one, and randomise a layer or everything except the note assignment. Keep a rhythm you like and change scale (e.g. to Phrygian) or root afterwards. Shorten gate if notes are too long. For Polyrhythm Studio: one Euclidean lane per scale degree with independent lengths.",
 ["DAW with a Euclidean generator script, or Web Audio / JS: up to 8 lanes, each a Bjorklund(pulses, steps) array rotated by its offset, mapped to scale[root + layerIndex] with per-lane velocity and gate (implementation note)","Any melodic synth or drum rack"],
 [("layers","list","Up to 8: {steps, pulses, rotation, velocity, gate}",True),("mode","enum","drums | melody",True),("scale","enum","Scale for melody mode (12 available in the video)",False),("root","note","Root note for melody mode",False)],
 [("clip","pattern","Generated MIDI pattern"),("layer_table","table","Layer settings and note assignment")],
 ["Randomise should not change note assignments, otherwise layers switch instruments or scale degrees.","Too many long gates in melody mode turn into a wash; reduce gate first.","Promo for the presenter's script: presets and download talk dropped; no factory presets copied."],
 [("Choose drum mode and assign layers to drum notes (kick, snare, closed hat, etc.). Enable two layers, set steps/pulses/rotation, and generate.","drum mode","A basic Euclidean groove in the clip"),
  ("Add layers one at a time (e.g. closed hat, crash), set each layer's velocity and gate, and regenerate.","add layers","A denser interlocking groove"),
  ("Change a layer's rotation or pulses (e.g. move the snare), or press randomise for one layer; use drift to move every active layer's pulse count by ±1.","variation","New grooves that stay related to the previous one"),
  ("Switch to melody mode, choose scale and root. Each layer now plays a different scale note; enable several layers with different step lengths.","melody mode","An in-key melodic pattern; coinciding hits form chords"),
  ("Shorten gate if notes overlap too much; randomise all (notes stay fixed) until a rhythm works.","shape notes","Clear, articulated in-key melody"),
  ("Keep the rhythm and change the scale or root (e.g. to Phrygian) and regenerate.","re-key","Same rhythm, new harmonic colour")],
 ["In melody mode, every generated note belongs to the chosen scale.","After drift, each active layer's pulses differ from before by at most one.","Changing the scale changes pitches but not hit positions."],
 ["euclidean","polyrhythm","generative","melody","scale-quantize","sequencer","midi","polyrhythm-studio","web-audio"],
 note="Script promo; Patreon/download and preset browsing dropped.")

mk("nbS1pT8V7YU","How to Create Classic House Pads (Any Synth!)",
 "classic-house-pad-ext-chords-16-voice-free-run-unison-width-vs-chorus-resonant-lp-plus-hp-slow-lfo-cutoff-slow-amp-noise-bypass-filter-chord-stab-loop-pad",
 "Classic house pad in any synth: minor 9 / minor 7 / major 7 chords, many-voice detuned unison with oscillator retrigger off (free-running), stereo spread from the unison width control or a chorus/ensemble if the synth has none, slightly resonant lowpass plus serial highpass to remove mud, slow LFO on cutoff, slow amp attack with no filter envelope, coloured noise partly bypassing the lowpass, delay + reverb sends; plus a quick pad from a looped chord-stab sample",
 "Build the classic house pad and know which ingredient does what. Use extended chords (minor 9, minor 7, major 7, major 9 and inversions). Turn unison up (up to 16 voices per oscillator, or 32), detune until it is wide and airy but not seasick, and give a second oscillator a different detune so voices do not sit on top of each other. Turn oscillator retrigger off so voices run free; with retrigger on, voices start in phase and just phase against each other. If unison does not spread voices in stereo, use the width control or add a chorus (an ensemble chorus suits pads). Set a lowpass cutoff a little laid-back with some resonance for character, cut the lows with a highpass (in series), and move cutoff with a slow LFO. Use a slow amp attack and full sustain, and remove any filter envelope. Try pulse waves and PWM by LFO, a sub oscillator for weight, and coloured noise at low level fed partly around the lowpass so its highs survive. Finish with delay and reverb sends. Quick alternative: loop a steady section of a chord-stab sample with a crossfade and slow attack, then hide the loop with delay, reverb and a filter.",
 ["Any polyphonic synth with unison/detune, retrigger option, lowpass and highpass filters, LFO and noise, or Web Audio: per voice 8–16 detuned OscillatorNodes with random start phases (free-run), StereoPannerNode spread, BiquadFilter lowpass→highpass, slow LFO → frequency (implementation note)","Chorus/ensemble, delay and reverb","Sampler with loop crossfade (for the chord-stab variant)"],
 [("chords","list","Extended chords: m9, m7, maj7, maj9, inversions",True),("unison_voices","integer","8–16 per oscillator (up to 32 if available)",True),("detune","number","Wide and airy, below obvious out-of-tune",True),("width_source","enum","unison width | chorus/ensemble",True)],
 [("house_pad","patch","Classic house pad patch"),("chord_stab_pad","patch","Optional looped chord-stab pad")],
 ["Retrigger on (all voices start in phase) causes phasing instead of chorusing; turn it off.","A chorus sounds different from true unison spread; choose per synth.","Too much detune sounds out of tune; back off until airy.","Plugin names from the video are not required; no preset pack or sample pack content used. Values were described, not given numerically, except voice counts."],
 [("Program extended chords (e.g. Em9, Em7, Emaj7) on an init saw patch with the filter open.","chords","Dense, warm harmonic base"),
  ("Raise unison toward 16 voices and detune until wide and airy; add oscillator 2 with its own unison at a slightly different detune.","unison","A thick chorused sound"),
  ("Turn off oscillator retrigger so unison voices free-run.","free-run","No phasing at note start; continuous chorus motion"),
  ("If voices are not spread in stereo, raise the unison width/spread control; if the synth has none, add a chorus/ensemble after it.","stereo spread","A wide pad instead of a mono chorus"),
  ("Set the lowpass cutoff a little low with some resonance; add a highpass in series to remove muddy lows; add a slow LFO on cutoff.","filters","Laid-back, band-limited pad with gentle movement"),
  ("Set a slow amp attack with full sustain and remove filter-envelope amount.","envelopes","Pad fades in smoothly without a filter blip"),
  ("Optional colour: switch to pulse or mix pulse and saw, modulate pulse width with a slow LFO, add a sub oscillator; add coloured noise at low level, routing some of it straight to the highpass/output so its highs bypass the lowpass.","colour","Vintage air and extra weight"),
  ("Add delay and reverb on sends and check against drums.","space","Spacious pad sitting behind the drums"),
  ("Chord-stab variant: load a chord stab into a sampler, loop a steady section after the transient (snap off), add loop crossfade and slow attack, then hide the repeat with delay, reverb and a filter.","sampler pad","A usable pad from a one-shot chord")],
 ["With retrigger on vs off, the free-run version has no phase-aligned 'zip' at chord start.","Raising unison width changes the image from centred to wide (check with a correlation meter or by soloing L/R).","The highpass audibly removes low mud without thinning the chord.","The noise layer is audible as air above the lowpass cutoff."],
 ["pad","house","unison","detune","free-run","stereo-width","chorus","filter","noise","chords","web-audio"],
 note="Walkthrough across several synths; sample-pack and course promotion dropped.")

mk("bIbOgZn1h_Y","Field Recording a Piano - Exploring Resonance & Sustain",
 "piano-resonance-multimic-contact-plus-air-mics-blend-sampler-then-paulstretch-fft-size-tonal-noise-extreme-stretch-and-sample-to-wavetable-patches",
 "Record a piano for maximum sustain and resonance with a contact/geophone pickup (about 10 Hz–1 kHz) at the most resonant spot plus air microphones inside and above the body for the highs, build a sampler with per-mic blend and a lowpassed 'muffled' mix, then make ambient textures by extreme time-stretching held notes and tails (FFT size: small = grainy, large = smooth; tonal vs noise emphasis) and reuse samples as wavetables, looped dual-sample pads and phaser-hollowed basses",
 "Turn an acoustic piano (or any resonant instrument) into an intimate sample set and ambient source material. Test contact-pickup positions on the body and choose the one that gives the longest, most natural sustain (in the video: the bottom-left corner beside the lowest key; a metal plate inside sounded resonant but metallic). Because the contact pickup captures only about 10 Hz–1 kHz, add air mics: one inside the closed lower body and one on the lid near the keys. Record each note several times, letting it ring fully (low notes 40–50 s), plus the key release. Denoise, align all mics, and build a sampler with a per-mic volume mix; lowpassing the full mix to the contact-pickup range gives a fuller, more sustained muffled piano than the pickup alone. For sound design, extreme-stretch held notes, chords and tiny leftover tails (30 s to over 20 minutes) and adjust FFT size (small = grainy, large = smooth) and tonal-vs-noise emphasis, pitch-shifting results. Reuse samples as wavetables: 16-voice unison with zero phase randomisation, an extreme many-pole phaser with high feedback for hollowness, slow spectral/granular scanning, and two loops of the same note with slightly different loop lengths panned L/R so the beating speeds up at higher pitches.",
 ["Contact microphone or geophone, plus one or two wide-range recorders/mics","Audio editor with denoise; sampler with per-group volume","Extreme time-stretch tool (Paulstretch or similar FFT stretch), wavetable/granular synth; in Web Audio, a phase-vocoder AudioWorklet or offline stretch, and two AudioBufferSourceNodes with different loopEnd panned L/R (implementation note)"],
 [("instrument","object","Piano or other resonant instrument",True),("pickup_positions","list","Candidate contact-mic spots to test",True),("stretch_factor","number","Output length vs input (30 s → 23 min in the video)",False),("fft_size","enum","small (grainy) … large (smooth)",False)],
 [("multimic_sampler","instrument","Sampler with contact/inside/lid mic blend"),("stretched_textures","audio","Extreme-stretched ambient pads and drones"),("derived_patches","patch","Wavetable, looped-pair pad and phaser bass from the samples")],
 ["Contact pickups miss the highs; without air mics the top octaves lose pitch and only key clicks remain.","Align mics sample-accurately before mapping, or blends will comb-filter.","Not affiliated with any vendor; the presenter's paid sample packs and presets are not used. Mic and plugin models from the video are examples only."],
 [("Attach the contact pickup at several spots and play the same notes; choose the spot with the longest natural sustain (bottom corner next to the lowest key in the video).","pickup placement","One position clearly sustains longer and sounds most natural"),
  ("Add air mics: one inside the closed lower body, one on the lid near the keys, to recover highs above about 1 kHz.","air mics","High notes keep their pitch and clarity"),
  ("Record every note several times, letting each ring out fully, and record the key-release noise too.","recording","Long sustains (tens of seconds on low notes) and release samples"),
  ("Denoise, chop, and align all mic tracks; map them into a sampler with a volume control per mic, plus a mix lowpassed to about the contact-pickup range.","sampler build","Blendable dry/intimate to natural piano, and a fuller muffled preset"),
  ("Extreme-stretch held notes, chords and short tails with an FFT stretch tool. Try small FFT sizes for grain and large for smoothness; shift tonal vs noise emphasis; pitch-shift the results.","extreme stretch","Ethereal pads and drones many minutes long"),
  ("Load samples as wavetables: try 16 unison voices with zero phase randomisation; for a bass, add an extreme phaser (many poles, high feedback) and a 'muted' lowpass macro.","wavetable patches","Digital-piano tones and hollowed-out basses"),
  ("Make a pad or bass from two loops of the same note with slightly different loop lengths, panned left and right, blended with a resampled wavetable and drive.","looped pair","Stereo beating whose speed rises with pitch")],
 ["The chosen pickup spot gives a measurably longer sustain than other spots on the same note.","Soloing the contact mic vs the air mics shows the pickup's high-frequency roll-off around 1 kHz.","Changing FFT size from small to large audibly moves the stretch from grainy to smooth.","In the looped pair, the beating rate increases on higher notes."],
 ["field-recording","sampling","contact-mic","paulstretch","time-stretch","ambient","wavetable","phaser","sound-design","web-audio"],
 note="Sampling/sound-design walkthrough; Patreon and sample-pack promotion dropped.")
